            {"index": 8, "type": "descriptive", "text": "Explain the raw materials and setting process of Portland Cement in construction.",
             "options": None, "correctAnswer": "Raw Materials: Limestone (calcium carbonate), clay (silica and alumina), and gypsum (calcium sulphate dihydrate). Setting Process: When cement is mixed with water, chemical hydration reactions start forming interlocking crystals of calcium silicates and aluminates, creating a hard, rock-like mass. Gypsum is specifically added to retard the initial setting rate so masons have enough time for mixing and bricklaying.", "marks": 5},
            {"index": 9, "type": "descriptive", "text": "Describe the preparation of soap by saponification and explain how soap cleans dirty greasy clothes.",
             "options": None, "correctAnswer": "Preparation: Vegetable oil (like coconut or castor oil) is heated and thoroughly stirred with sodium hydroxide (caustic soda) solution. The reaction between fatty acids and alkali produces soap and glycerol (saponification). Common salt is added to precipitate out the soap. Cleansing action: Soap molecules have two ends: a hydrophilic (water-loving) ionic head and a hydrophobic (oil-loving) hydrocarbon tail. In wash water, the tails attach to oil/grease and heads remain in water, forming spherical micelles that trap the dirt and rinse it away.", "marks": 5},
