            {"index": 7, "type": "short", "text": "What is an Operating System (OS)? State its main function.",
             "options": None, "correctAnswer": "An Operating System is system software that acts as an interface between the computer hardware and the user, managing hardware resources and enabling application programs to run.", "marks": 2},
            {"index": 8, "type": "descriptive", "text": "Explain how hardware and software work together in a computer system using a real-world analogy.",
             "options": None, "correctAnswer": "Hardware and software are interdependent: hardware cannot perform any operation without software instructions, and software cannot execute without physical hardware. Real-world analogy: A car and a driver. The car body, steering wheel, and engine represent the hardware. The driver's skills, knowledge of traffic rules, and driving decisions represent the software. Without a driver (software), the car (hardware) is just an inert machine.", "marks": 5},
            {"index": 9, "type": "descriptive", "text": "Classify computer software into System Software and Application Software with detailed examples and their roles.",
             "options": None, "correctAnswer": "1. System Software: Directly controls hardware and manages system resources. Examples: Operating Systems (Windows, macOS, Linux, Android) which manage memory, files, and devices; Device Drivers that communicate with printers/webcams. 2. Application Software: Designed to carry out specific user tasks. Examples: Word processors (MS Word) for typing documents, Media players (VLC) for playing audio/video, Web browsers (Chrome) for surfing the internet, Graphic tools (Paint) for drawing.", "marks": 5},
